.TH XRDCP 1 "2026-07-06" "brix client" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xrdcp \- copy files between local, root://, WebDAV/HTTP and S3 endpoints
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xrdcp
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fISRC\fR... \fIDST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xrdcp
copies files and directory trees between local paths, \fBroot://\fR /
\fBroots://\fR servers, WebDAV/HTTP(S) endpoints and S3 buckets, with
resilient transfers (reconnect + resume), checksum verification,
third-party copy, parallel jobs and streams, sync/mirror semantics and
resumable batch manifests.
.PP
\fISRC\fR and \fIDST\fR may be a \fBroot://host[:port]//path\fR URL,
a web URL (\fBdavs://\fR, \fBhttp(s)://\fR, \fBdav://\fR,
\fBs3://\fR, \fBs3s://\fR), a GridFTP URL (\fBgsiftp://\fR, \fBftp://\fR),
a local path, or \fB\-\fR (stdin/stdout).
Web-to-web copies relay through a local temporary file.
Multiple sources, a glob, or \fB\-\-from\fR imply that \fIDST\fR is a directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Overwrite an existing destination.
.TP
.B \-r
Recursively copy a tree (root/davs/http/s3 <-> local, or web<->web).
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-posc
Persist-on-successful-close (upload).
.TP
.B \-s
Silent: suppress all non-error output.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-d ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-\-debug
Verbose / debug output.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " FILE"
Read sources from a manifest (one per line; \fB\-\fR reads from stdin).
.TP
.BI \-\-journal " PATH"
Record completed transfers to \fIPATH\fR; skip already-completed entries on the next run.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Shorthand for \fB\-\-journal\fR \fI<manifest>.journal\fR (requires \fB\-\-from\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-retry " N"
Retry each failed transfer up to \fIN\fR times with exponential back-off; 0 = fail fast.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-retry
Disable transport resilience: fail immediately on the first fault.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-stall " MS"
Reconnect/resume patience window in milliseconds (0 = fail fast).
Also settable via \fBXRDC_MAX_STALL_MS\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-refresh
Proactively renew an expired or near-expiry token (via oidc-agent) or GSI proxy
before transferring.
.TP
.BI \-\-oidc\-account " NAME"
oidc-agent account for \fB\-\-auto\-refresh\fR (or \fB$OIDC_ACCOUNT\fR).
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " \fIN\fR"
Copy up to \fIN\fR files concurrently (batch mode).
.TP
.B \-\-sync
Skip transfers whose destination already has the same size (or mtime/checksum
per \fB\-\-sync\-check\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-sync\-check " MODE"
Comparison method for \fB\-\-sync\fR: \fBsize\fR (default), \fBmtime\fR, or
\fBcksum\fR[:\fIalgo\fR].
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be transferred or deleted; move no bytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PAT"
Skip files matching this fnmatch pattern (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-include " PAT"
Only transfer files matching this fnmatch pattern (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-delete
With \fB\-r \-\-sync\fR: delete destination entries that are absent from the source.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-source
Delete each source file after its transfer succeeds (local and root:// sources only).
With \fB\-\-sync\fR, files that are already up\-to\-date (skipped) are \fInot\fR removed.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-no\-progress
Suppress the progress bar even when stderr is a TTY.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show a transfer progress bar with ETA (enabled automatically on a TTY for single-file copies).
.TP
.B \-\-verify
After each transfer, verify the checksum against the server (root:// only).
.TP
.B \-\-tls
Require in-protocol TLS (implied by \fBroots://\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-notlsok
Permit cleartext if the server offers no TLS (root:// only).
.TP
.B \-\-noverifyhost
Skip TLS hostname verification (certificate chain validation stays on).
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " PROTO"
Force an authentication protocol: \fBgsi\fR | \fBztn\fR | \fBkrb5\fR |
\fBsss\fR | \fBunix\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " PATH"
Use \fIPATH\fR as the X.509 proxy certificate, overriding \fBX509_USER_PROXY\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-pgrw
Use paged I/O (\fBkXR_pgread\fR/\fBkXR_pgwrite\fR) with per-page CRC32c integrity.
.TP
.BI \-\-io\-uring " MODE"
Local-disk overlap ring: \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR | \fBauto\fR (default \fBauto\fR).
Overrides \fBXRDC_IO_URING\fR.  Also accepted as \fB\-\-io\-uring=\fImode\fR.
Any value other than \fBon\fR, \fBoff\fR, or \fBauto\fR is a usage error (exit 50).
.TP
.B \-\-io\-uring\-direct
Engage the O_DIRECT tier on the local-disk ring: the file descriptor is opened
with page-cache bypass and the ring's buffers are block-aligned, so full-block
transfers move straight between disk and the ring.  The final short write of a
download is transparently re-issued as a buffered write.  Overrides
\fBXRDC_IO_URING_DIRECT\fR.  When the target filesystem rejects O_DIRECT an
\fBauto\fR ring falls back to the buffered tier and an \fBon\fR ring reports a
clean error.
.TP
.BI \-\-cksum " TYPE[:source|:print|:VALUE]"
Verify a checksum after transfer.
\fITYPE\fR is \fBadler32\fR, \fBcrc32c\fR, or \fBmd5\fR; the sha family
(\fBsha1\fR, \fBsha256\fR, \fBsha512\fR) is computed locally and therefore
valid only in \fB:print\fR and literal\-\fIVALUE\fR modes \(em the server
checksum plane has no sha support, so \fBsha*:source\fR is a usage error
rather than a silent pass.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " CODEC"
Enable root:// inline compression on download (read) and upload (write).
\fICODEC\fR: \fBgzip\fR | \fBdeflate\fR | \fBzstd\fR | \fBbr\fR |
\fBxz\fR | \fBbzip2\fR.
Server opt-in; silently ignored if the server does not support it.
.TP
.B \-\-zip
Store the local source as a STORE member of the destination ZIP archive
(overwrites the archive).
.TP
.B \-\-zip\-append
Like \fB\-\-zip\fR but append to an existing archive.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-streams " \fIN\fR"
Open N\-1 secondary \fBkXR_bind\fR data streams. Sub\-streams are on by
default (4); \fB\-S 1\fR disables them.
.TP
.B \-\-parallel
True concurrent striped download: one thread per data stream, each reading a
disjoint range. Opt\-in \(em the default serial fan\-out keeps the
single\-link resilient ride\-out, which this mode gives up.
.TP
.BI \-\-sources " N"
Extreme copy: download blocks from up to \fIN\fR replicas concurrently with
block stealing \(em when no unclaimed block remains, idle replicas re\-fetch
blocks still in flight on slower or dead ones, so the transfer finishes at the
fastest replicas' pace and survives a mirror dying mid\-transfer. Replicas
come from the metalink mirror list when the source is a
\fB.meta4\fR/\fB.metalink\fR, else from a \fBkXR_locate\fR on the source, else
the source URL is used \fIN\fR times. \fIN\fR is 1..16; 1 (the default)
keeps the single\-source paths. \fBBRIX_XCP_BLOCK\fR overrides the block size
in bytes (default 8 MiB, clamped to 64 KiB..64 MiB); \fBBRIX_XCP_DEBUG=1\fR
prints per\-source block accounting.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-metalink
Copy a \fB.meta4\fR/\fB.metalink\fR source as a plain file. By default such a
source is resolved as a metalink \fIvirtual redirector\fR: the document is
fetched (any transport) and parsed (RFC 5854 v4 and legacy v3), and the copy
fails over across its mirrors in rank order. Only remote pull schemes are
accepted as mirrors (root/roots/xroot/xroots/http/https/dav/davs); a document
digest of a supported type (md5, crc32c, adler32) acts as \fB\-\-cksum\fR
\fIalgo\fR:\fIhex\fR unless \fB\-\-cksum\fR was given explicitly.
.TP
.B \-X, \-\-xrate \fIRATE\fR
Cap the transfer rate at \fIRATE\fR bytes/sec (binary \fBk\fR/\fBm\fR/\fBg\fR
suffixes).
Applies to the serial path; mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-parallel\fR and
\fB\-\-sources\fR (which bypass the paced pump).
.TP
.B \-\-xrate\-threshold \fIRATE\fR
Fail the transfer when the average rate drops below \fIRATE\fR bytes/sec
(3\ s grace so connection setup never trips it).
.TP
.B \-\-xattr
Preserve \fIuser\fR-namespace extended attributes across a
\fBroot://\fR-to-local copy (stock: "preserve extended attributes").
Best-effort: a failed attribute warns and the copy still succeeds.
For safety only the \fBuser.\fR namespace ever crosses \(em
\fBsystem.\fR, \fBsecurity.\fR and \fBtrusted.\fR names are skipped in
both directions, so a remote server cannot plant kernel metadata
(capabilities, ACLs) on a local file.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-coerce
Set \fBkXR_force\fR on the remote destination open (stock: "ignore file
locking semantics").
BriX\-Cache servers have no mandatory usage\-locking to override and accept
the bit; the flag matters for drop\-in scripts and stock destinations that
enforce usage rules.
.TP
.BI \-\-retry\-policy " force|continue"
What a \fB\-\-retry\fR attempt does with the partial destination:
\fBforce\fR restarts from scratch (the default), \fBcontinue\fR arms the
\fB\-\-continue\fR byte\-offset engine so every attempt (including the
first) writes the destination in place and each retry resumes at the
partial's size.
.TP
.B \-\-rm\-bad\-cksum
Accepted for stock compatibility.
Stock deletes a checksum\-mismatched destination only when this is given;
BriX\-Cache does so unconditionally (fail\-closed default), so the flag is a
no\-op alias of the stricter behavior.
.TP
.B \-\-continue
Resume an interrupted download at the existing partial destination's size
(stock xrdcp semantics).
The destination is written \fIin place\fR \(em no atomic temp+rename \(em so a
failed transfer leaves the partial for the next \fB\-\-continue\fR; a local
file already larger than the source is refused.
A \fIcompleted\fR file that then fails \fB\-\-cksum\fR is still removed
(fail\-closed on integrity verdicts).
Mutually exclusive with \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-pgrw\fR, \fB\-\-compress\fR, the
\fB\-\-zip\fR modes, and \fB\-\-journal\fR/\fB\-\-resume\fR (a different
resume system).
.TP
.BR \-\-tpc " " \fBfirst\fR " | " \fBonly\fR " | " \fBdelegate\fR
Server-side third-party copy (remote to remote): the destination pulls the
bytes from the source directly, so no data transits this client.
.RS
.TP
.B first
Try TPC; on failure fall back to a client-mediated copy.
.TP
.B only
TPC or hard fail.
.TP
.B delegate
Like \fBonly\fR, but the destination reads the source \fIas you\fR rather than
with its own service identity. This emits \fBtpc.dlgon=1\fR and arms the
client to answer the destination's X.509 delegation round (\fBkXGS_pxyreq\fR)
by signing it with your proxy, advertising that capability during the GSI
handshake so a conformant destination knows to ask.
.RE
.IP
Delegation is never implicit: signing the peer's request hands it a credential
that speaks as you, so it happens only under \fB\-\-tpc delegate\fR (or with
\fB$XRDC_GSI_DELEGATE\fR set, for tools with no copy-options block). A
destination that asks for delegation on a session that did not enable it is
refused with a message naming both ways to opt in.
.TP
.BI \-\-tpc\-token\-mode " MODE"
Bearer-token forwarding mode for \fB\-\-tpc\fR: passed as \fBtpc.token_mode\fR
in the opaque query string (nginx-xrootd extension; silently ignored by other servers).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-token " \fIJWT\fR"
WebDAV/HTTP bearer token (or \fB$BEARER_TOKEN\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-s3\-access " KEY"
S3 SigV4 access key ID (or \fB$AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-s3\-secret " KEY"
S3 SigV4 secret key (or \fB$AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-s3\-region " REGION"
S3 SigV4 region (or \fB$AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR; default \fBus\-east\-1\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-wire\-trace [= N ]
Decode every frame to stderr; \fIN\fR>=2 adds a hex dump.
.TP
.B \-\-timing
Print per-opcode round-trip time at the end.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print this help.
.SH URLS
.TP
.BR root [ s ]:// host [: port ]// path
Native XRootD protocol; default port 1094.
The double slash separates the connect endpoint from the server-side path.
\fBroots://\fR enables in-protocol TLS (same as \fB\-\-tls\fR).
An alias in \fI~/.xrdrc\fR may replace the \fIhost[:port]\fR component.
.TP
.BR davs:// " | " dav:// " | " https:// " | " http:// host [: port ]/ path
WebDAV/HTTP endpoint.
\fBdavs://\fR and \fBhttps://\fR enable TLS;
\fBdav://\fR and \fBhttp://\fR are cleartext.
.TP
.BR gsiftp:// " | " ftp:// host [: port ]/ path
GridFTP endpoint; default port 2811 for \fBgsiftp://\fR, 21 for \fBftp://\fR.
\fBgsiftp://\fR authenticates the control channel with GSI (RFC 2228
\fBAUTH GSSAPI\fR/\fBADAT\fR) using the X.509 proxy named by
\fB\-\-proxy\fR or \fBX509_USER_PROXY\fR, falling back to
\fI/tmp/x509up_u<uid>\fR, and delegates it to the server;
the trust store comes from \fB\-\-ca\-dir\fR or \fBX509_CERT_DIR\fR.
A \fBgsiftp://\fR endpoint is never downgraded to an anonymous login: a
missing or unusable proxy fails the copy.
\fBftp://\fR logs in anonymously with no protection and is meant for testing.
Data moves over a passive channel (\fBEPSV\fR, falling back to \fBPASV\fR);
the client refuses a data address that is not the control peer, and always
refuses a privileged data port.
Third-party (\fBgsiftp\fR to \fBgsiftp\fR) and recursive copies are not
supported by this client — use the server's TPC surface for those.
.TP
.BR s3:// " | " s3s:// host [: port ]/ bucket / key
S3 SigV4 endpoint.
\fBs3s://\fR enables TLS.
Credentials from \fB\-\-s3\-access\fR / \fBAWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fR and
\fB\-\-s3\-secret\fR / \fBAWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Recursive mirror with deletion of stale destination entries
xrdcp -r --sync --delete root://se//data/run42/ /scratch/run42/

# Batch transfer from a manifest, resuming a previous interrupted run
xrdcp --from list.txt --resume -j 8 root://se//dest/

# Single-file download with checksum verification
xrdcp --verify root://store.example:1094//atlas/file.root /tmp/
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XRDC_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
Connect and protocol bring-up timeout in milliseconds.
.TP
.B XRDC_IO_TIMEOUT_MS
Per-operation I/O timeout in milliseconds once a session is established.
.TP
.B XRDC_MAX_STALL_MS
Reconnect/resume patience window (same as \fB\-\-max\-stall\fR).
.TP
.B XRDC_IO_URING
Default io_uring mode for local-disk I/O: \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR | \fBauto\fR (default \fBauto\fR).
.TP
.B XRDC_IO_URING_DIRECT
Default O_DIRECT tier for the local-disk ring: \fBon\fR/\fB1\fR enables page-cache
bypass, anything else leaves it off (default off).  Overridden by \fB\-\-io\-uring\-direct\fR.
.TP
.B XRDRC
Path to the configuration file (default \fI~/.xrdrc\fR).
.TP
.B BEARER_TOKEN
Default WebDAV/HTTP bearer token (same as \fB\-T\fR).
.TP
.B X509_USER_PROXY
GSI proxy certificate path (used by \fB\-\-auth gsi\fR, \fBgsiftp://\fR and
\fB\-\-auto\-refresh\fR).
.TP
.B BRIX_GSIFTP_ALLOW_OFFPEER
Set to \fB1\fR to let a GridFTP server steer the data channel to an address
other than the control peer.  Off by default because an attacker-controlled
reply would otherwise turn the client into an FTP-bounce relay; the privileged
port refusal still applies with it set.
.TP
.B OIDC_ACCOUNT
Default oidc-agent account for \fB\-\-auto\-refresh\fR.
.TP
.B AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
S3 access key (same as \fB\-\-s3\-access\fR).
.TP
.B AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
S3 secret key (same as \fB\-\-s3\-secret\fR).
.TP
.B AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
S3 region (same as \fB\-\-s3\-region\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.xrdrc
Per-user configuration file defining endpoint aliases and default credentials.
See
.BR brix\-env (7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on full success; non-zero if any transfer failed.
On error, the exit code is mapped as follows:
50 on a usage error (EUSAGE).
51 on socket/connect/timeout error (ESOCK), permanent name-resolution failure (ERESOLVE),
or data corruption (EINTEGRITY, I/O-error class).
52 on redirect loop/budget exhausted (EREDIRECT) or malformed server frame (EPROTO).
53 on authentication failure (EAUTH; several error classes share this code with syscall
errors like EPERM, EACCES when running against native XRootD).
54 on unsupported feature (EUNSUPPORTED) or generic server error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xrdfs (1),
.BR xrdcksum (1),
.BR xrddiag (1),
